Title: The Cistern
Order: 1
Production Code: 1x01

FADE IN:

EXT. WINCHESTER, VIRGINIA - MORNING (1867)

An antiquated town near the northwestern corner of the State of Virginia.

SUPER: "WINCHESTER, VIRGINIA. OCTOBER 1867."

INT. PIEDMONT COTTAGE - MORNING

A low, squatty house built of rock. One room, a loft above it with the hole to climb into in plain view. One window, four feet square, two panes broken out and stuffed with rags. A bed in the corner. A trundle bed beneath it. Three chairs, two of them without backs.

Hannah Piedmont sits in one of them. Before her stands eight-year-old Belton Piedmont, dressed for school except that his face is not washed. His trousers are two torn pairs made into one: a leg of linen sewn to a leg of navy blue. His coat is a conglomeration of patches. One foot is in a worn-out woman's slipper, the other in a turned-over farm boy's boot. His hat is a summer straw.

Around them: JAMES HENRY (15), AMANDA ANN (13), ELIZA JANE (11) already gone to school, and little CELESTINE (5), too young to go. Their father has long since abandoned the family.

Hannah holds a wet cloth. Belton has a ring of bacon grease around his lips and no wish to mix with cold water.

HANNAH PIEDMONT
Cum er long hunny an' let yer mammy fix yer 'spectabul, so yer ken go to skule. Yer mammy is 'tarmined ter gib yer all de book larning dar is ter be had eben ef she has ter lib on bred an' herrin's, an' die en de a'ms house.

A hint about a certain leather strap and the offer of a lump of sugar bring him to terms.

EXT. WINCHESTER COLORED SCHOOL - DAY

A long, plain frame structure painted white. Once a church for the white Baptists, condemned as unsafe; the roof patched, iron rods holding the twisting walls together. A large open field on every side.

On the top step, four feet up, a white man rings a little hand bell, calling children in from recess. They run in helter skelter. This is Tiberius Gracchus Leonard.

Hannah and Belton reach the foot of the steps. An angry scowl crosses Leonard's face. He mutters half aloud:

TIBERIUS GRACCHUS LEONARD
Another black nigger brat for me to teach.

To show his disgust he draws back so that Hannah will pass without brushing him. He draws back too far. He goes off the end of the steps, clutching at the door, and the children turn in their seats just in time to see his feet where his head ought to be.

A yell of laughter, savage and spontaneous. The teacher is plainly not beloved.

The back of his head has struck the edge of a stone. He climbs up with his back covered in blood, walks down the aisle glaring right and left, takes his hat, and goes to a drug store to have his wounds dressed.

INT. WINCHESTER COLORED SCHOOL - CONTINUOUS

Hannah and Belton take seats midway the room. The children take charge. ABE LINCOLN sits in the teacher's chair and plays "'fessor."

ABE LINCOLN
Sallie Ann ain't yer got wax in yer mouf?

SALLIE ANN
Yes sar.

ABE LINCOLN
Den take dis stick and prop yer mouf opun fur half hour. Dat'll teach yer a lesson.
(to another)
Billy Smith, yer didn't know yer lessun. Yer may stan' on one leg de ballunce ob de ebenning.

He takes down the cowhide and passes to and fro, flogging pretend offenders.

ABE LINCOLN (CONT'D)
Henry Jones, yer sassed a white boy ter day. Pull off yer jacket. I'll gib yer a lessun dat yer'll not furgit soon. Neber buck up to yer s'periors.

ABE LINCOLN (CONT'D)
John Jones, yer black, nappy head rascal, I'll crack yer skull if yer doan keep quiut.

ABE LINCOLN (CONT'D)
Cum year, yer black, cross-eyed little wench, yer. I'll teach yer to go to sleep in here.

Each sally brings a roar of laughter; he imitates the teacher perfectly. Behind him BILLY SMITH creeps to the teacher's chair and sets a crooked pin in it.

Leonard's face appears at the door. All scramble to their places. Billy has "the cold sweats."

Leonard does not go to his seat. He approaches Hannah and Belton.

TIBERIUS GRACCHUS LEONARD
What is your name?

HANNAH PIEDMONT
Hannah Lizabeth Piedmont.

TIBERIUS GRACCHUS LEONARD
Well, Hannah, what is your brat's name?

HANNAH PIEDMONT
His name am Belton Piedmont, arter his grandaddy.

TIBERIUS GRACCHUS LEONARD
Well, Hannah, I am very pleased to receive your brat. He shall not want for attention.

It is accompanied by a lurking look of hate that makes Hannah shudder and long to have her boy at home again. Her desire for his schooling is greater. She lets him stay.

A rustling at the door. Leonard turns, and is petrified.

On the threshold, holding a boy by the hand, stands a mulatto woman, tall and graceful, raven-haired, with brown hazel eyes tinged with melancholy and a rich olive complexion. This is Fairfax Belgrave. The boy is Bernard Belgrave.

Leonard pulls off the hat he has not removed since the drug store. He is taken with stage fright. He escorts them to the front and gives them seats.

TIBERIUS GRACCHUS LEONARD (CONT'D)
(suave)
What is your name?

FAIRFAX BELGRAVE
Fairfax Belgrave.

TIBERIUS GRACCHUS LEONARD
May I be of any service to you, madam?

She colors slightly at the word "madam."

FAIRFAX BELGRAVE
I desire to have my son enter your school and I trust that you may see your way clear to admit him.

TIBERIUS GRACCHUS LEONARD
Most assuredly madam, most assuredly.

He hastens to his desk, opens it, takes out his register, sits, and leaps several feet in the air, knocking over the desk. He dances around the floor, reaching for the rear of his pants.

TIBERIUS GRACCHUS LEONARD (CONT'D)
Pull it out! Pull it out! Pull it out!

The children hide behind their books. Abe rolls on the floor. Billy Smith is struck dumb with terror.

Leonard extracts the pin and stands scratching his head before his charming visitor. Inwardly he swears to be revenged on Hannah and her son, the innocent means of his double downfall.

He rights his desk and takes up his pen.

INSERT - THE REGISTER

"Bernard Belgrave, age 9 years." "Belton Piedmont, age 8 years."

EXT. PIEDMONT COTTAGE - SUNDAY

The family in its best. Belton and James Henry at the biscuit pan; the promise of a biscuit and a piece of chicken once the preacher has done. The PARSON, small, head to one side like a parrot, shiny-black trousers two inches too short, a Prince Albert coat, a beaver hat, a cane, has accepted Hannah's invitation to dinner.

INT. PIEDMONT COTTAGE - LOFT - DAY

Belton lies on his stomach at the hole, James Henry at a knothole above the table. Below, the parson eats.

BELTON
(whispering)
James, dees haf gone.

A moment.

BELTON (CONT'D)
He's tuck anudder.

One biscuit left. The parson reaches. Belton, overcome, tumbles through the hole to the floor.

BELTON (CONT'D)
(kicking, crying)
I knowed I wuzunt goin' to git naren dem biscuits.

The parson gives him the last biscuit and passes a five-cent piece round to each child to make peace. From the loft:

JAMES HENRY
Amanda Ann you git mine fur me.

INT. PIEDMONT COTTAGE - LATER

The children dismissed. Hannah and the parson alone at the table. She has asked him whether she should take Belton out of school, given the teacher's hatred.

THE PARSON
Let me gib yer my advis, sistah Hannah. De greatest t'ing in de wul is edification. Ef our race ken git dat we ken git ebery t'ing else. Dat is de key. Git de key an' yer ken go in de house to go whare you please. As fur his beatin' de brat, yer musn't kick agin dat. He'll beat de brat to make him larn, and won't dat be a blessed t'ing? See dis scar on side my head? Old marse Sampson knocked me down wid a single-tree tryin' to make me stop larning, and God is so fixed it dat white folks is knocking es down ef we don't larn. Ef yer take Belton out of school yer'll be fighting 'genst de providence of God.

Hannah decides. On Monday, Belton goes back.

INT. WINCHESTER COLORED SCHOOL - A MONTAGE OF YEARS

-- Leonard buys flowers from the florist for Bernard to carry home to his mother. Belton is given fresh bruises to carry home to his.

-- Leonard invites Bernard to dine. He finds a pretext to forbid Belton to eat his own common meal.

-- Bernard climbs on the teacher's knee, plays with his mustache, takes his watch and wears it. Belton sees. The other children, except Belton, begin to envy and despise Bernard.

-- Belton recites. Leonard brings all his talents to bear on his failures, to exalt Bernard. Belton learns to be scrutinized. Bernard does not gain an inch on him.

-- The boys, older, declaiming heroes: Leonidas, Marco Bozzaris, Louis Kossuth, Robert Emmett, Patrick Henry.

SUPER: "TWELVE YEARS."

Leonard is still the teacher. He has decided to graduate the young men and to use the day as a lasting humiliation of Belton and exaltation of Bernard.

EXT. WINCHESTER - NIGHT

Belton slips from the congressman's residence, leaving the neighborhood of the school with a spade in one hand and a few carpenter's tools in the other. The Congressman, a polished orator with a national reputation, reads Belton's oration in his library and opens his eyes wide in astonishment.

INT. THE WOOD-SHED - NIGHT

Belton is fire-maker for the school; the care of the wood-shed has fallen to him alone. A hole runs from the shed floor to a point under the school-room platform.

INT. UNDERGROUND CAVITY - NIGHT

A rock-walled room that will hold water. Dirt from the digging has gone into a deep old unused well. Directly under the spot where the teacher always stands, Belton has cut a square hole from beneath, the boards cut almost through. A prop with a weight attached holds the cut section up; the weight rests in a hole in the wall near the top.

A strong rope runs from the weight beneath the schoolhouse floor to a point under Belton's seat, where an auger hole brings the end through.

For two or three nights Belton works until nearly morning filling the cistern with water.

The Cistern Trap is set.

INT. WINCHESTER COLORED SCHOOL - COMMENCEMENT DAY

The colored men and women have laid aside all work. The forward seats are reserved for the white people: the congressman, the mayor, the school trustees and their wives and daughters.

At the foot of the program, two names. BELTON PIEDMONT. BERNARD BELGRAVE.

Leonard has given Bernard the last place, deeming that the more advantageous. The judges: the mayor and two school trustees, the congressman having politely declined and named another trustee in his stead.

Belton walks up on the platform. The children greet him with applause.

BELTON
The Contribution of the Anglo-Saxon to the Cause of Human Liberty.

In his strong, earnest voice he begins to roll off his periods. The whole audience seems in a trance. Time and again they burst into applause. When he has finished, white men and women crowd to the front to congratulate him. It is many minutes before quiet is restored.

Bernard takes his position.

BERNARD
Robert Emmett.

His voice is sweet and well modulated. More than once tears come into the eyes of his hearers, especially at the description of Emmett's death.

Belton returns to his seat to await the proper moment. The judges retire.

INT. WINCHESTER COLORED SCHOOL - OUTSIDE THE DOOR

The judges cannot decide. They beckon the teacher out.

TRUSTEE
That black nigger has beat the yellow one all to pieces this time, but we don't like to see nigger blood triumph over any Anglo-Saxon blood. Ain't there any loop-hole where we can give it to Bernard, anyhow?

TIBERIUS GRACCHUS LEONARD
(eagerly)
Well, yes, on the ground of good behavior.

MAYOR
There you hit it. So we all decide.

INT. WINCHESTER COLORED SCHOOL - COMMENCEMENT DAY

The judges file in. The Mayor rises.

MAYOR
We award the prize to Bernard Belgrave.

VOICES
(all over the house)
No! no! no!

The congressman rises, goes up to Belton, and congratulates him on his triumph over oratory, and laments his defeat by prejudice. A perceptible stir passes through the audience.

Leonard takes a large gold medal from his desk and takes his accustomed place on the platform.

TIBERIUS GRACCHUS LEONARD
Ladies and Gentlemen, this is the proudest moment of my life.

Belton, in his seat, has found the rope. He pulls.

BELOW THE PLATFORM

The weight drops, the prop is pulled, the board gives.

ABOVE

Leonard goes down into a well of water.

LEONARD (O.S.)
Murder! Murder! Murder! Help! Help! Help! I am drowning. Take me out, it is cold.

The audience rushes forward. They find him standing apparently unharmed in a cistern, the water a little more than waist deep. Their fright gives way to humor and a merry shout goes up from the scholars. The colored men and women laugh to one side. The white people smile as at a fine specimen of falling from the sublime to the ridiculous.

The larger students bend over the well and lift him out by the arms. He stands wet and shivering, water dripping from his hair. The medal is gone.

He dismisses the audience, draws his last month's pay, and leaves that night for parts unknown.

CUT TO:
